WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.430
Hi everyone, this is Jerry.

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:06.430
This is a game from the 1972 World Chess Championship
between Bobby Fischer and Boris Spassky.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:12.379
It's a Round 10 game, Fischer had the white
pieces and opened with e4, "best by test"

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:14.019
as he would say.

00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:20.110
Spassky plays e5, knight f3, knight c6, and
bishop to b5.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.660
So this indicates the Ruy Lopez or Spanish
game.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.290
Pressure on the knight, indirect pressure
on the pawn.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:29.780
I'll just be showing some alternative variations.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:34.390
There is a lot to know with the Ruy Lopez,
there is a lot of theory on it.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.600
I'll just be touching on some what-ifs.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.670
In the game we saw a6, but what if knight
f6?

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:42.670
Pressure against e4.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:48.699
We could see white simply castling and black
opting to go for the Berlin Defense, grabbing

00:00:48.699 --> 00:00:56.319
on e4 and the following sequence of moves
coming about where the end result looking

00:00:56.319 --> 00:01:02.520
on the black side and black's deficiencies,
is that the black king is uncastled and has

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.920
a little messed up pawn structure, but as
compensation has the bishop pair in an open

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.830
position which is usually an asset.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.050
So that's a little trade-off that would occur
from that position.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:19.260
But we don't see that, but instead a6 chases
the bishop back to a4.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.971
One other quick note, if you try to grab this
pawn like this, queen d4 is played, these

00:01:22.971 --> 00:01:25.690
two are hit, the pawn will be regained.

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:28.320
So that's why we see bishop back.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.790
And now knight f6, hitting e4.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:36.110
White simply ignores that threat and castles
in this game.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.450
Bishop e7 was played.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.220
But again what if knight takes pawn?

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.410
This indicates the Tarrasch variation.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:47.070
Can this pawn be taken for free without black
getting killed?

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:52.510
Well, the answer is no but you have to know
how to react to that sort of move.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:59.450
d4 to just try and open up this position at
a moment where white is ahead in development

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.950
and the black king has yet to castle.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.970
This e-file is really going to come into play
in this sort of variation.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:11.400
b5, bishop b3, and now an important point
in this position as black.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.630
You can't be greedy, you can't just go ahead
and grab pawns like this.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:20.290
That'll be met with rook e1 and you are in
an uncomfortable pin.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:25.370
How to really meet this threat against your
knight which is unprotected and pinned?

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:30.650
d5 can be met sharply with knight c3, and
if you grab my knight, I'm going to grab the

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:36.109
pawn, and for those of you who are jujitsu
fans you'll know that black from this position

00:02:36.109 --> 00:02:37.889
will be "tapping out" very shortly.

00:02:37.889 --> 00:02:43.579
These two knights are hit directly, this rook
is hit indirectly all by just one white piece,

00:02:43.579 --> 00:02:45.730
and this knight is really just doomed.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:49.359
He's really caught in a crossfire, hit by
both the bishop and rook.

00:02:49.359 --> 00:02:53.760
So it's just bad news for black from this
position, not to be recommended going into

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.249
this specific variation.

00:02:56.249 --> 00:03:01.249
However from this point right here instead
of taking the pawn we could see simply d5,

00:03:01.249 --> 00:03:07.219
a much better response opting to allow the
bishop to come to this e6 square as well as

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:10.769
allow this pawn to support this e4 knight.

00:03:10.769 --> 00:03:16.859
After pawn takes pawn, just to summarize it
very briefly: the material has been restored

00:03:16.859 --> 00:03:21.700
and it's just a much more playable position
for sure.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:28.920
We don't see this, we don't see the Tarrasch
variation but instead black opts for bishop

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.480
to e7, more development.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.430
And it is at this point that black is maybe
getting ready to really grab that pawn and

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.829
hang onto it.

00:03:36.829 --> 00:03:42.650
So only at this point here does white choose
to go ahead and defend this pawn.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:47.569
What's interesting to note is that there are
other ways to go ahead and watch over e4.

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:52.879
You could play d3, watches over e4, you could
play knight c3.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:59.260
Both of these outlined moves that I have in
blue watch this over e4 pawn, so why aren't

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.030
they played?

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:06.530
In the Ruy Lopez, it's important for you to
know what sort of plan to implement, what

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:11.290
sort of middle game strategy should you try
to undertake.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:16.280
Knowing this thing will allow you to know
why rook to e1 is the preferred move from

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:18.910
such a position.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:25.870
Here is what you want to do as white: you
want to make a move like pawn to c3, followed

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.389
by pawn to d4.

00:04:27.389 --> 00:04:32.610
You want to establish two pawns in the center
on these two specific squares and "maintain"

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.560
them on those two squares for as long as you
can.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:41.389
And I emphasize "maintain" for a very important
reason which I will get to later.

00:04:41.389 --> 00:04:48.750
c3 is played for a couple reasons, again to
support this d4 advance as well as provide

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:52.360
a cubbyhole for this bishop on the c2 square.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:54.340
And taking that into account...

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:58.259
if that's what we want to do, it stands to
reason that that's why we shouldn't make a

00:04:58.259 --> 00:05:05.289
move like knight to c3, because we want to
have our pawn here, and we don't play pawn

00:05:05.289 --> 00:05:12.129
to d3 because we don't want this pawn to play
the role of a defensive piece.

00:05:12.129 --> 00:05:16.509
Its aspirations are to get to d4 where it
is an aggressive piece putting pressure on

00:05:16.509 --> 00:05:18.419
this e5 pawn.

00:05:18.419 --> 00:05:22.050
So with that in mind, that's why we're seeing
the move rook to e1.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:27.849
One additional benefit of this rook move to
e1 is that f1 is vacated so that this sort

00:05:27.849 --> 00:05:31.629
of knight type of maneuver is possible.

00:05:31.629 --> 00:05:39.599
It's often the case that in this opening the
position will close up, and when that occurs,

00:05:39.599 --> 00:05:44.290
maneuvering type of moves just in general
are more likely to occur.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:48.349
And so you could see the knight take this
sort of journey where it starts on b1 of course

00:05:48.349 --> 00:05:54.979
and it ends up on g3 with its ideal square
focusing on this f5 point, a soft point in

00:05:54.979 --> 00:05:57.319
the black position.

00:05:57.319 --> 00:05:59.509
So those are some things to be aware of.

00:05:59.509 --> 00:06:03.759
This is why we are playing rook here because
we want to take into account what white's

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:09.590
plan is going to be and we're also vacating
f1 for our b1 knight.

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:14.599
At this point white is actually getting ready
to go ahead and chop on c6 and then on e5

00:06:14.599 --> 00:06:16.770
and simply be up pawn.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:21.139
If black castles at this point we could just
go ahead and go for this.

00:06:21.139 --> 00:06:25.479
And notice how this move here, queen d4 it's
not the same.

00:06:25.479 --> 00:06:30.720
White has clearly done some more development
prior to grabbing the pawn.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.419
There is no queen takes pawn from this variation
the rook is of course watching over it.

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:43.230
So after rook to e1, now we see this pawn
to b5 move chasing the bishop to b3.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.419
And now black opts to play d6.

00:06:46.419 --> 00:06:54.030
What's interesting to me is why d6 is played
here opposed to just castling.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:07:00.979
Castling will allow black to at least consider
going into the Marshall Attack.

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:06.990
For example: if c3 is played here black could,
you know if you are an aggressive player as

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:14.139
black you could be making this pawn to d5
move, giving up pawn in exchange for pressure

00:07:14.139 --> 00:07:16.979
against the white king.

00:07:16.979 --> 00:07:22.259
These are the moves that you would see played:
c6, a move like bishop d6, the queen could

00:07:22.259 --> 00:07:28.160
jet out to h4 to try and induce some pawn
move for white...

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:34.240
try to induce some sort of pawn advance which
creates a weakness against a white king who

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.560
is lacking defensive pieces.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.940
More specifically, look at the queen side,
just take a quick glance, none of them have

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:42.280
been moved.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.810
So white is lagging in development.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:51.060
So that's really where a lot of the compensation
lies from such a variation.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:56.310
We're not seeing this but again that's just
something that kind of interests me here after

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:01.770
bishop b3... why not just simply castle and
at least reserve the option to play d6 or

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:02.770
d5.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:10.120
But nevertheless what we see is just d6 here,
and now c3 and only then castle.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:15.289
So keeping in mind what we want to do, we
want to play this d4 move, however we don't

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:20.220
want to play it just yet, and there is a big
reason why.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:24.949
If we play d4 right now, what black can do
is play bishop g4.

00:08:24.949 --> 00:08:30.600
And the word that I was previously emphasizing
is this word "maintained".

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:35.440
Not only do we want to establish two pawns
on d4 and e4 but we want to be able to maintain

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:37.479
them there for as long as we can.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:44.970
There is a big reason for this, and it's because
this pawn on d4 is the one who could really

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:48.670
change the pawn structure whenever it wants.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:53.360
It could capture on e5 or it could push forward.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:09:00.320
Or let me just back up... after bishop g4
it could capture on e5 and open up the position,

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:05.250
or it could opt to play d5 and close up the
position.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:10.370
Being in that sort of set up where you have
the option to really change the pawn structure...

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:16.680
to open the position up, or to close the position
really makes a big deal.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:22.620
Because for as long as you keep black guessing
what you're going to do, it's very difficult

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:26.110
for black to meet those sorts of ideas.

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:33.480
You know, will white open up the position
or is white going to close up the position?

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.750
We really want to keep black guessing for
as long as we can.

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:43.500
But if we play d4 right away like this, bishop
g4 will do what's referred to as "resolve

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.560
this tension in the center".

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:49.899
This tension cannot be maintained because
of the pressure that's now being placed on

00:09:49.899 --> 00:09:50.950
the white position.

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:58.070
More specifically this knight on f3 is a critical
defender of this d4 square, and with this

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:04.430
bishop not only getting ready to take this
knight... for example let me just play a nothing

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:09.180
type of move if a3 is played, if bishop takes
knight we can't recapture with the queen because

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:13.839
we're going to lose on d4, and if we capture
like this well notice how we've weakened our

00:10:13.839 --> 00:10:15.810
own king side structure.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:22.829
So in other words, it's important to insert
this move right here for white, instead of

00:10:22.829 --> 00:10:28.620
d4 right away, we want to play h3 to prevent
this bishop from coming here because again,

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:32.220
not only do we want to establish pawns here
in the center, but we want to maintain them

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.800
there for as long as we can.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:40.380
So, knight b8 seems a bit awkward.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:44.329
That goes into what's known as the Breyer
variation, knight to b8.

00:10:44.329 --> 00:10:50.660
It's intention is to reposition itself on
this d7 square, have the knights connected

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:54.170
and also it allows for this c5 advance.

00:10:54.170 --> 00:11:00.920
So notice for as long as the knight is on
c6 this pawn can of course not move at all.

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:08.220
So the knight move allows this pawn on c7
to advance where it could then meet this,

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:13.150
you know challenge this pawn that white is
going to be placing on d4 very soon.

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:20.040
One other variation, black and go for knight
a5, and bishop here followed by c5.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:22.760
We could see d4, queen c7.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:25.050
This is another way to go about things.

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:26.250
But we are not seeing that.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:34.699
Instead the Breyer variation, knight b8 followed
by knight b to d7 will be in effect.

00:11:34.699 --> 00:11:40.280
And only now d4, so finally we've made all
the prep moves necessary, c3, h3, and only

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:43.250
now d4.

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:46.500
Knight b to d7, essentially a developing move
right...

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:49.329
underdeveloped and now we have to get it back
into play.

00:11:49.329 --> 00:11:53.269
Knight b to d2 and bishop to b7.

00:11:53.269 --> 00:12:01.279
Notice how by putting pressure immediately
on this point right here, black is restraining

00:12:01.279 --> 00:12:02.279
this d2 knight.

00:12:02.279 --> 00:12:06.180
I was mentioning earlier that this knight
would like to get to f1, maybe g3.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:09.470
But we can't do that just yet, that pawn will
fall.

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:14.889
So by putting pressure on the white center,
we stop this sort of maneuver.

00:12:14.889 --> 00:12:21.880
Bishop back to c2, lends a supporter of this
e4 point and also allows this pawn to come

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:23.459
to b4.

00:12:23.459 --> 00:12:26.300
White is anticipating that black will be playing
c5.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:32.110
So white wants to get this b-pawn involved
in the fight for control of the center.

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:37.800
Rook e8 getting ready to tuck the bishop here
where the rook would then add potential pressure

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.769
against this e4 pawn.

00:12:40.769 --> 00:12:46.830
b4, bishop back allows again the rook to see
this pawn here, and now we see a4.

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:51.980
Again a move like knight to f1 can't be played
because after capture, this pawn is hit three

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:55.110
times and will be won next.

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:57.070
So knight to f1 is out.

00:12:57.070 --> 00:13:00.600
Instead we see pawn to a4, striking at this
pawn.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:05.750
This rook pawn is hitting a knight pawn, and
this a-file can potentially open up on white's

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:07.700
terms.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:14.940
Knight b6 looks to resolve this tension, and
Fischer opts to simply play a5.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:19.269
One other alternative, you know white can
go ahead and capture like this but I think

00:13:19.269 --> 00:13:24.660
black would be the side that's in a better
position to cope with the tension that is

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:28.120
now going on between these two rooks.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:34.589
Black can simply maybe make a move like queen
d7, and then the rooks would be in direct

00:13:34.589 --> 00:13:35.630
contact with one another.

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:38.079
For example: bishop b2, queen here.

00:13:38.079 --> 00:13:40.829
These rooks are in direct contact with one
another.

00:13:40.829 --> 00:13:44.610
The white queen is interfering with these
rooks for the moment.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:52.649
And you may very well see maybe a move like
knight to a4 causing maybe white some problems

00:13:52.649 --> 00:13:55.459
since it would be hitting this bishop and
pawn right here.

00:13:55.459 --> 00:13:59.579
You may very well see white having to give
this bishop up for the knight.

00:13:59.579 --> 00:14:04.279
So that's why we're maybe not seeing this
pawn exchange right here in this position,

00:14:04.279 --> 00:14:05.459
but instead a5.

00:14:05.459 --> 00:14:09.579
If the knight goes here that'll just run into
the loss of a pawn.

00:14:09.579 --> 00:14:13.649
So a5 kicks the knight back to d7.

00:14:13.649 --> 00:14:19.759
It may seem insignificant but the knight coming
to b6, a5, and then the knight going back

00:14:19.759 --> 00:14:23.389
to d7.

00:14:23.389 --> 00:14:30.259
Its main purpose was just to resolve the tension
that was going on between those two pawns.

00:14:30.259 --> 00:14:36.839
Bishop b2 gets the last minor piece involved,
and it looks a little bit awkwardly placed

00:14:36.839 --> 00:14:38.480
because it's just staring at its own pawn.

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:43.790
But in anticipation of this advance here,
soon enough this bishop will be able to see

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:47.750
the black position a little bit more clearly.

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:50.269
So queen b8 an interesting move.

00:14:50.269 --> 00:14:51.930
What's its intention?

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:56.090
To watch over this e5 pawn in a very indirect
way.

00:14:56.090 --> 00:15:01.720
There's some more work that needs to be done
before the queen is watching over e5 directly.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:08.310
This pawn will have to move, this pawn on
d6 will have to move, but this is the idea

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:12.020
that's in mind with this queen b8 maneuver.

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:15.830
Rook to b1, a little bit of an awkward move.

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:17.110
What is its purpose?

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:22.470
To watch over this bishop in anticipation
of what black will be wanting to do.

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.000
What does black want to do in this position?

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.300
He wants to get this c7 pawn involved.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:32.740
If black doesn't make that sort of move, what
more really is there in the position.

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:34.930
So what we do see is c5.

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:42.000
We have a series of exchanges, and soon enough
this bishop which looked dumb on b2 springs

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:43.000
to life.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:45.380
We have a bunch of exchanges going on on e5.

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:48.519
After the queen recaptures, pawn to c4.

00:15:48.519 --> 00:15:50.420
So what was the purpose of rook b1?

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:54.750
Watch over this bishop so it's protected and
this c4 move could come about.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.399
The queen is now hit.

00:15:56.399 --> 00:16:00.339
Queen f4, and now the bishop simply grabs
this knight.

00:16:00.339 --> 00:16:03.750
I'm not sure what else could be played here.

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:09.769
If pawn takes pawn, that could run into some
trouble with maybe this sort of pin.

00:16:09.769 --> 00:16:10.950
The knight is hit once.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.459
Maybe there is this pin going on.

00:16:13.459 --> 00:16:14.459
Right?

00:16:14.459 --> 00:16:19.300
How to really meet that threat, and maybe
this knight could grab here on the next turn.

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:24.199
So that's why we're not seeing that sort of
capture go on, pawn takes pawn because of

00:16:24.199 --> 00:16:29.220
the pressure that could be placed on this
d2 knight.

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:34.160
Instead bishop takes knight, queen takes bishop,
and only now pawn takes pawn.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:41.800
And rook on e to d8, a very sharp move looking
to, well first and foremost placing the knight

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:47.910
in a pin, but black is now getting ready to
place this queen very deep in the white position

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:56.959
where she really radiates, really shows herself
quite well hitting this knight again and also

00:16:56.959 --> 00:16:59.339
hitting this a5 pawn.

00:16:59.339 --> 00:17:06.420
So white simply gets out of that pin right
away, and queen c3 hits this knight for a

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:07.740
second time.

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:14.370
We can play this nice regrouping type of move,
moving to its most natural square a classical

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:16.789
square on f3.

00:17:16.789 --> 00:17:19.819
And after queen takes pawn we see bishop to
b3.

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:25.720
This is an interesting move, you know with
this tension between these two pawns you may

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:30.840
very well be tempted as white to maybe just
grab this pawn here but Fischer is starting

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:37.860
to look at this king here and how this point
on f7, this pawn on f7 is a bit vulnerable.

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:43.470
If you could attack it once and maybe a second
time that starts to be an issue and sure enough

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:48.820
in this game pressure starts to mount against
this pawn right here on f7.

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:56.350
So let's start out first with bishop b3, places
that pawn in a pin, and after pawn takes pawn

00:17:56.350 --> 00:17:57.770
we have queen to f4.

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:03.539
So this pawn is hit twice, some calculation
was of course needed with this sequence of

00:18:03.539 --> 00:18:05.960
moves bishop b3, queen f4.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:07.289
What calculation exactly?

00:18:07.289 --> 00:18:11.640
Well you have to know how to cope with this
move here of course.

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:17.460
Normally if there weren't any tactics in this
position, this c4 move would just slam a door

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.130
in this bishop's face.

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:23.590
But we could just capture like this and then
the rook is there to just come to the seventh

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:27.720
rank and cause some more problems for black.

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:33.960
Queen f4 is played, this move is out of the
question and now we have rook d7 adding a

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:36.000
supporter.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.860
Knight e5 attacking the rook and the pawn.

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:41.789
So we are just piling up on f7 at this point.

00:18:41.789 --> 00:18:47.000
The queen comes back to defend f7 indirectly
and place the knight in a pin against the

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:48.910
queen.

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:50.950
And now we have rook b to d1.

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:53.740
This is a very important point in the game.

00:18:53.740 --> 00:18:57.460
We can't just make any rook move.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:19:02.390
This rook on e1 is contributing in some way
to the position; it's watching over e4.

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:07.620
This rook, it's only intention previously
was to watch over a bishop that was on b2.

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:13.690
It has finished its duty in doing that, so
it's time to get that rook involved.

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:16.600
That's why we're seeing rook b to d1.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:23.210
And after rook to e7, we have a series of
exchanges occurring on f7, and we quickly

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:27.250
arrive at an endgame position which is unbalanced.

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:29.480
We have these two pawns which are together.

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:33.280
They are connected passed pawns.

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:38.960
And we end up having an unbalanced position
where Fischer is able to show some really

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:42.679
good technique in bringing the point home.

00:19:42.679 --> 00:19:47.910
Bishop takes pawn, rook takes bishop, so a
series of exchanges, and at this point bishop

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:48.910
takes pawn here.

00:19:48.910 --> 00:19:53.950
If king takes knight, we just have rook check
and grab the bishop.

00:19:53.950 --> 00:20:03.250
So taking on e4 first will allow black to
gain additional material prior to grabbing

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:04.680
on f7.

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:10.630
So bishop takes, rook takes, king takes and
now rook on the seventh giving a check, and

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:11.630
king to f6.

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:15.860
So essentially what you want to do as white
in this position is restrain these guys from

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:16.870
pressing forward.

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:22.080
So ideally the rook came here not to just
check, but it really wants to get to this

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:23.080
b7 square.

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:29.159
It wants to get behind these pawns to prevent
them from pressing forward.

00:20:29.159 --> 00:20:37.900
So rook b7, rook check, king h2, bishop d6
check, g3, and now b4.

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:43.380
So finally the threat rook takes b5 was met.

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:45.570
And now what do we do with the endgame?

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:48.240
We get our kings involved, king g2.

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:50.610
h5, I'm not quite sure what that does.

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:55.330
Maybe just prevents this g4 move from coming
about.

00:20:55.330 --> 00:21:00.140
It's really difficult to suggest good moves
for black in this position because these white

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:01.809
rooks are placed very well.

00:21:01.809 --> 00:21:05.529
They are really stopping the black pawns from
advancing.

00:21:05.529 --> 00:21:10.610
Neither move could come about without the
loss of material for black.

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:15.920
So just rook b6 really just trying to tie
black up as best you can.

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:20.090
The bishop is now pinned so the rook moves
there to defend.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:24.400
King f3, king back to get out of the pin,
king e2.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:26.660
This rook is lacking squares.

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:35.379
It really could only go to d5 to still be
in direct contact with the bishop, and f4.

00:21:35.379 --> 00:21:37.950
We have a three versus two majority.

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:42.460
So we have to get these three pawns rolling,
so that's why we're seeing this advancement

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:43.460
f4.

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:46.130
We are going to see g4 as well.

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:48.090
In the meantime what can black do?

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:52.770
Not a whole lot, in fact this rook right here
doesn't have any reasonable moves.

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:56.090
Any moves would result in the loss of material.

00:21:56.090 --> 00:22:00.280
So black has to resort to this sort of pawn
move.

00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:03.870
Black is very close to just being in zugzwang.

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:11.370
I guess there could be a move like bishop
back here but really nothing concrete is coming

00:22:11.370 --> 00:22:13.779
about with the black moves here.

00:22:13.779 --> 00:22:21.640
So g6, g4, we have the exchange of pawns,
and now g5 looking to just grab the last of

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:23.340
the white pawns.

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:27.810
Maybe after something like this, maybe if
black could grab this pawn here at some point.

00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:31.320
Of course this bishop is hanging but ideally
what black would like to do in this position

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:36.559
is really just trade all of the white pawns
and just get into an ending where it's the

00:22:36.559 --> 00:22:38.540
rook and bishop versus the two white rooks.

00:22:38.540 --> 00:22:41.850
That's a much more drawable sort of position.

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:46.931
So in response to g5, Fischer wants to maintain
those pawns on the board so that's why we

00:22:46.931 --> 00:22:49.570
see f5.

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:54.840
Bishop to e5 preventing this rook from maybe
making use of e6.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:23:01.980
In fact there could very well be threats like
rook b7, and if the king comes here that would

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:04.750
actually run into mate with rook to e6.

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:12.929
So the bishop to e5 move stops the rook from
accessing these squares here.

00:23:12.929 --> 00:23:19.779
So rook b5 pins this pawn to the rook which
allows this move right here rook takes pawn.

00:23:19.779 --> 00:23:23.450
And black is pretty much in a bind right here.

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:26.539
The move played was king to f6, allowing rook
takes pawn.

00:23:26.539 --> 00:23:29.640
But there really wasn't a way to avoid it.

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:34.500
For example if you move this bishop, we could
see rook check, king here.

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:35.580
That's really the only move right?

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:40.210
Because if you go here you get mated and if
you go here you get mated.

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:44.289
So this is the only move from this position.

00:23:44.289 --> 00:23:49.760
And actually what we would see from here is
a move like king to d3.

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:52.309
Notice how we don't have to worry about this
discovered check.

00:23:52.309 --> 00:23:53.779
The bishop can go wherever it wants.

00:23:53.779 --> 00:23:55.429
We have all our pieces on light squares.

00:23:55.429 --> 00:23:57.679
This guy is not going to bother us at all.

00:23:57.679 --> 00:24:02.760
So the king can kind of just shift over and
alleviate this rook's duty of watching over

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:04.200
this pawn from advancing.

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:08.210
So this king can come to c2 and watch over
this pawns advancement.

00:24:08.210 --> 00:24:13.700
And in the meantime this rook could eventually
come over in this direction and maybe threaten

00:24:13.700 --> 00:24:17.730
to come here at some point, maybe get ready
to press forward on f6.

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:22.100
So things like that are in the air if black
isn't careful.

00:24:22.100 --> 00:24:29.040
So considering those sorts of ideas that white
has, black is opting to just allow this sort

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:30.909
of capture to occur.

00:24:30.909 --> 00:24:36.890
So king f6, rook grabs the pawn and now bishop
to d4.

00:24:36.890 --> 00:24:40.640
Grabbing the rook going into this endgame
is simply lost.

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:45.779
Pawn here, rook here, pawn here, and the king
is just going to sneak over here and just

00:24:45.779 --> 00:24:47.250
grab this pawn next.

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:51.289
These two pawns right here are connected.

00:24:51.289 --> 00:24:56.110
So it's a big asset in almost all pawn endings.

00:24:56.110 --> 00:24:58.720
This ending right here would be no exception.

00:24:58.720 --> 00:25:05.070
So we don't see that sort of exchange after
rook pawn, simply bishop d4.

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:10.990
White throws in a check here, king to e5,
and now a sharp move right here by Fischer,

00:25:10.990 --> 00:25:13.110
king to f3 threatening mate.

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:16.160
There is no time to grab this rook because
of rook e6.

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:18.430
So how to meet that?

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:24.100
The rook simply retreats allowing the king
to make use of d5 in the event of a check.

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:27.110
And now white offers these rook exchanges.

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:32.360
This is a good strategy... if you are up material,
offer some exchanges.

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:35.270
So that's what we have with this sequence
of moves.

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:40.669
And once these rooks are on this position
right here, now we have this possibility where

00:25:40.669 --> 00:25:45.600
the rook can come to g6, getting ready to
grab him and then it's just going to be bad

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:46.760
news for black.

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:53.559
So finishing up we have king d5, the rook
grabs the pawn, bishop e5, f6 pins the bishop

00:25:53.559 --> 00:25:55.039
to the king.

00:25:55.039 --> 00:25:57.820
And after king to d4, we have rook to b1.

00:25:57.820 --> 00:26:01.130
And from this position black resigns, and
for good reason.

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:05.029
How to really meet this threat right here
where the rook is getting ready to just check

00:26:05.029 --> 00:26:06.940
the king and then grab the rook?

00:26:06.940 --> 00:26:10.919
There is really no convenient way to do it
because this king unfortunately from this

00:26:10.919 --> 00:26:14.679
position is tied down to having to defend
this bishop here.

00:26:14.679 --> 00:26:17.010
So if something like this occurs...

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:22.660
just showing you one variation if bishop takes
pawn, we could simply see, really just pick

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:27.190
your favorite variation, this would be one
of them: rook check, king here.

00:26:27.190 --> 00:26:31.091
We could just simply grab this pawn and then
grab this rook, and this is going to be a

00:26:31.091 --> 00:26:32.200
won game.

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:42.200
So as it went in this game, after king to
d4 and rook to b1, Spassky ends up resigning.

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:43.200
So that's all for this video.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:45.289
Hope you are able to take something away from
it.

00:26:45.289 --> 00:26:46.049
Take care, bye.

